    "response": {
      "reply": "Here's my useful first pass on the proposed equipment-loan policy.\n## Useful first pass\n**What the policy covers (as reported):** Employees at the US office may borrow a laptop for 14 days. The policy is internal and doesn't currently state who can approve an extension.\n**The core gap you flagged is the right one.** An equipment-loan policy that allows a 14-day loan but is silent on extension approval creates two practical problems:\n1. **Unclear authority \u2192 inconsistent outcomes.** Without a named approver, extensions get granted ad hoc (or not at all), which is unfair and hard to audit.\n2. **No accountability for the asset.** A laptop is company property; an open-ended extension with no owner means no one is responsible for tracking return, condition, or data security (e.g., a departing employee holding a device).\n**What the policy should ideally specify around extensions** (beyond just naming an approver):\n- Whether extensions are permitted at all, and if so, a cap (e.g., one extension of up to 14 more days) so the \"14-day\" rule isn't effectively meaningless.\n- Who approves \u2014 typically the employee's direct manager (for business need) plus IT/equipment (for asset tracking), or a single owner.\n- What happens on overdue return (escalation, recovery, data wipe).\n**The single most decision-changing missing fact** is who is authorized to approve an extension. That's the gap you asked me to identify, and it's the one I'd resolve first.\n**Note on scope:** This is an internal employment/asset-management matter, not a customer-facing or regulatory one, so no external authority is needed for the first pass. No legal decision or draft is requested yet, so I've stopped at analysis.\n---\nTo move this forward, I have a few questions. The most important one first:\n**1. Who should be authorized to approve an extension of the 14-day laptop loan period?**\n- Employee's direct manager\n- IT / equipment team\n- HR\n- Any of the above (manager, IT, or HR)\n- Not yet decided \u2014 this is the gap to fill\n**2. Should extensions be permitted at all, and if so, should there be a cap?** *(optional)*\n- No extensions \u2014 14 days is firm\n- One extension allowed, with a defined additional period\n- Extensions allowed without a stated cap\n- Not yet decided\n**3. Does the policy cover only laptops, or other equipment as well?** *(optional)*\n- Laptops only\n- Laptops and other equipment (e.g., monitors, phones, peripherals)\n- Not yet decided",
      "conversation_id": "CONV-20260905-95d1c0",
